.TH TIMED 8
.SH NAME
timed \- wall clock, SNTP discipline and time status
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B timed
.RB [ -d
.IR dir ]
.RB [ -s
.IR server ]
.RB [ -i
.IR seconds ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B timed
owns the wall clock.  It takes the time from the hypervisor when there
is one, from SNTP otherwise, and disciplines it thereafter.
.PP
There are two clocks.  The wall clock is
.BR /dev/time :
microseconds since the epoch, writable by the host owner, and shared by
every namespace.  A write sets it exactly, sub-second part included, so
it can be moved by less than a second at a time.  The monotonic clock is
.BR millisec (2)
in the kernel (milliseconds since boot) and
.BR clock_gettime (2)
under the emulator; it is never set, and timeouts should use it.
.PP
The clock source on 9vm is the host's own.  The guest has no RTC, so the
hypervisor serves
.B /n/host/time
as a virtual file: microseconds since the epoch, formatted when it is
read.  (The service reads
.B /dev/time
either side of that request and takes the midpoint, because the round
trip through the host filesystem can take a tick.)  At start it sets
.B /dev/time
from it and then measures it again every second: an error over one
second is stepped, a smaller one is handed to the slew described below.
If the host stops publishing the file, or the guest has no host
filesystem, SNTP is the source and a clock still at 1970 stays wrong
until a server answers.
.PP
Servers come from
.B -s
options or from
.BR /lib/ndb/time ,
one
.B sntp=\fIhost\fP
line per server.  If neither is given, three public anycast servers are
tried by address (the guest may not have a resolver yet): NIST
.BR 132.163.96.1 ,
Cloudflare
.BR 162.159.200.1 ,
and Google
.BR 216.239.35.0 .
The query runs in its own process.  If a server never answers, that
process remains blocked and no further attempts are made until
restart; the boot clock stays in place and status keeps being
published.  A reachable server answers immediately.  After a successful
exchange it computes offset and round-trip delay from the four
timestamps and steps
.B /dev/time
when the offset exceeds one second, or when the clock has not yet been
verified by SNTP.  A smaller offset is removed a fraction at a time:
every second it moves the clock by one eighth of what remains, capped
at 100 ms, so the wall clock never jumps and second-resolution users
see it run slightly fast or slow until the error is gone.
.PP
The service republishes
.BI dir /status
every second:
.PP
.RS
.EX
source=host|sntp
synced=1
verified=0|1
server=S
offset_us=N
delay_us=N
stratum=N
last=unixseconds
now=microseconds
mono=milliseconds
pending_us=N
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B pending_us
is the part of the last measured offset still to be slewed.
With the host source,
.I server
is
.BR host ,
.I stratum
is 0,
.I verified
is 0 (nothing vouches for the host's clock but the host), and
.I delay_us
is the round trip through the host filesystem.  With SNTP,
.I verified
is 1 and
.I delay_us
is the four-timestamp round trip.
.I source
is empty until some source has been read.
.B now
and
.B mono
are written when the file is published, not live; use
.B /dev/time
and
.B millisec
for the current values.
.SH OPTIONS
.TF "-i seconds"
.TP
.BI -d " dir"
Publish directory (default
.BR /n/time ).
.TP
.BI -s " server"
Add an SNTP server; may be repeated.
.TP
.BI -i " seconds"
Seconds between syncs (default 900).
.SH FILES
.TF /lib/ndb/time
.TP
.B /dev/time
wall clock, microseconds since the epoch
.TP
.B /n/host/time
host epoch seconds on 9vm
.TP
.B /lib/ndb/time
.B sntp=
lines
.TP
.B /n/time/status
last published state
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/timed.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR millisec (2),
.IR sys (2)
.SH BUGS
A step is still used for an offset over one second or for the first
SNTP correction, and it can move the wall clock backwards.  The slew
only starts after a measurement, so a drift that accumulates between
syncs is corrected at the next one; at 100 ms per second a one-second
error takes about fifteen seconds to remove.  Leap seconds, NTP
authentication and server selection by stratum are not implemented; SNTP replies are accepted
from the first configured server that answers.  A blackholed server
leaves one query process blocked and disables further SNTP attempts
until restart.  The default list is
unauthenticated and reveals the guest's address to those operators;
use
.B /lib/ndb/time
to choose your own.  Google's public time is leap-smeared, so it is
listed last.  A reply is checked for
the client's origin timestamp but the server is not authenticated,
so this is only as trustworthy as the network path.
